course: biology
lesson: 29
title: Crocodiles and alligators
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Crocodiles and alligators are reptiles with four short legs, a long tail and a body that has hardly changed since their ancestors lived alongside the dinosaurs over 200 million years ago, because it is a perfect shape for hiding in water and ambushing prey.
explain: Their eyes, ears and nostrils sit on top of the head, so a crocodile can lie in a river with only those showing, breathing, watching and listening while the rest of it is hidden like a floating log.
explain: To tell them apart, look at the snout and the teeth: an alligator has a wide U-shaped snout and its lower teeth hide when it shuts its mouth, while a crocodile has a narrower V-shaped snout and a big lower tooth still sticks out.
explain: In Peter Pan the crocodile swallows a ticking clock and chases Captain Hook for more; real crocodiles can't chew at all, so they spin over and over in the death roll to twist off chunks and swallow them whole.
explain: And the cute pictures of a capybara sitting on a «crocodile» are usually a caiman in South America, a relative of alligators that has often just eaten and doesn't bother with a big rodent.
fact: Alligators have a wide U-shaped snout and hidden lower teeth, while crocodiles have a narrower V-shaped snout and a lower tooth that shows when the mouth is shut.
fact: A saltwater crocodile has one of the strongest bites ever measured, but the muscles that open the jaws are weak, so experts can hold a small one's mouth shut with tape.
fact: A crocodile's eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of its head, and a clear third eyelid protects its eyes under water like goggles.
fact: Crocodiles do make tears while eating, to keep their eyes wet, not because they are sad; «crocodile tears» means pretending to be sad.
fact: Crocodiles lay eggs in nests; the mother guards them and carries the hatchlings to water in her mouth.
fact: The temperature of the nest decides whether the babies will be male or female.
fact: Crocodiles are cold-blooded: they bask in the sun to warm up and gape their mouths to let heat escape, and can go months without eating, because an animal that does not burn food to keep warm uses much less energy.
fact: Crocodile ancestors lived alongside dinosaurs over 200 million years ago, and crocodiles are the closest living relatives of birds.
fact: The saltwater crocodile is the largest living reptile, with big males over 6 metres long, and it can travel by sea between islands.
fact: In the death roll a crocodile spins in the water to tear chunks off its prey, because it can't chew.
fact: American alligators live in swamps, rivers and lakes in the south-eastern USA and recovered from being endangered once hunting was controlled.
fact: Alligators bellow so deeply that the water on their backs dances, and babies call from inside their eggs when ready to hatch.
fact: In the dry season alligators dig gator holes that keep water when everything else dries out, and many other animals drink and live there.
fact: Caimans, South American relatives of alligators, often let capybaras, the world's biggest rodents, rest beside them or on their backs.
exercise: What shape is an alligator's snout? | A wide U-shape | A narrow V-shape | A long thin beak | A flat square
exercise: Which one still shows a big lower tooth when its mouth is shut? | The crocodile | The alligator | Both of them | Neither of them
exercise: What is special about a saltwater crocodile's bite? | It is one of the strongest bites ever measured | It is poisonous, like a snake's bite, and kills at once | It is weaker than a dog's | It can bite only under water
exercise: Why can experts hold a small crocodile's jaws shut with tape? | The muscles that open the jaws are weak | Crocodiles fall asleep when touched | The tape they use is much stronger than steel | Crocodiles have no teeth when small
exercise: Why can a crocodile hide with only its eyes and nostrils showing? | Its eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of its head | It breathes through its eyes | It can hold its breath for ever | Its nostrils are at the very end of its long tail, like a snorkel
exercise: What protects a crocodile's eyes under water? | A clear third eyelid, like goggles | Thick eyelashes | A layer of mud | Nothing at all, it just shuts its eyes tight under water
exercise: Why does a crocodile make tears while eating? | To keep its eyes wet, not because it is sad | Because it feels sorry for the animal it is eating | Because the food is too spicy | Crocodiles never make tears
exercise: How does a mother crocodile take her babies to the water? | She carries them gently in her mouth | She rolls them down the bank | She carries them on her back and tail | She leaves them to find it alone
exercise: What decides whether baby crocodiles will be male or female? | The temperature of the nest | The size of the mother | The month in which they hatch | The colour of the eggs
exercise: Why does a crocodile lie on a riverbank with its mouth wide open? | To let heat escape and cool down | To catch flies with its tongue | To show the others it is about to fall asleep | To scare away the sun
exercise: Which animals lived alongside the ancestors of crocodiles over 200 million years ago? | Dinosaurs | People | Mammoths | Dogs and cats
exercise: Which living animals are the closest relatives of crocodiles? | Birds | Lizards | Snakes | Turtles
exercise: Which is the largest living reptile? | The saltwater crocodile | The Komodo dragon | The American alligator | The reticulated python of Asia
exercise: Why does a crocodile spin over and over in the water while holding prey? | To tear off chunks, because it can't chew | To make itself dizzy and brave before it eats | To wash its prey clean | To swim faster to the bank
exercise: Where do American alligators live? | In swamps, rivers and lakes of the south-eastern USA | In the warm seas all round the coast of Australia and New Zealand | In the rivers of India | In the Sahara desert
exercise: What happens on an alligator's back when it bellows? | The water on its back bounces and dances | Its scales light up | Its back turns red | Steam rises off its hot back
exercise: The capybara on a reptile's back is usually sitting on what? | A caiman, a South American relative of alligators | A Nile crocodile in Egypt | A saltwater crocodile in Australia | A Komodo dragon
test: in peter pan the croc swallows a clock and wants to chew up captain hook. what's wrong with that? | Real crocodiles can't chew; they tear off chunks and swallow them | Real crocodiles only eat clocks | Nothing, crocodiles chew like we do | Real crocodiles chew their food slowly with big flat back teeth, like cows
test: Count them: four crocodiles are basking on a bank. How many legs is that? | 16 | 8 | 12 | 20
test: A friend says "she's crying crocodile tears". What does she mean? | She's pretending to be sad | She's really very sad | She has an eye infection | She's eating very fast
test: Two tanks: one animal has a broad round snout and no lower teeth showing. Which is it? | The alligator | The crocodile | It could be either | Neither, it's a lizard
test: What would happen to a nest of crocodile eggs if the weather got much hotter? | More of the babies would be one sex than the other | All the eggs would hatch into lizards | The eggs would all hatch a whole year early, in the middle of winter | Nothing, temperature never matters
test: oi mate fancy a swim in that river? sign says crocs | No, never swim where the signs warn about crocodiles | Yes, crocodiles only live in the sea | Yes, if you splash they leave | No, but you can paddle in the shallow water right near the bank
test: Why can a crocodile go months without eating, but you can't? | Cold-blooded animals use much less energy | Crocodiles eat stones instead | Crocodiles store extra food in their thick tails for later | You could too if you tried
test: Mate reckons birds and crocodiles aren't related at all. Who's right? | Crocodiles are birds' closest living relatives | Your mate is right, they're not related in any way | Crocodiles are closest to snakes | Birds are closest to frogs
test: Why might a capybara feel safe beside a resting caiman? | A caiman that has just eaten may not bother with a big rodent | Caimans are afraid of capybaras | Capybaras are poisonous to eat | Caimans eat only plants
test: Why did American alligators recover after being endangered? | Hunting was controlled | They learned to live in the sea | People fed them every day | They started laying more eggs in cities
test: During a drought, why are gator holes good for other animals? | The holes the alligators dig keep water when everything else dries out | The alligators share their food | The holes are full of fruit that falls from the trees around them | Alligators leave in droughts
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between the snout of an alligator and that of a crocodile? | The alligator's is broad and U-shaped; the crocodile's is narrower and V-shaped | The alligator's is narrow; the crocodile's is broad and square | Both are identical in every respect | The crocodile possesses a beak
exam: Identify the factor which determines the sex of crocodile hatchlings. | The temperature at which the eggs are incubated | The age of the mother | The month of laying | The depth of the river in which the mother lives
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which a crocodile is observed to rotate repeatedly in the water while grasping its prey. | It is incapable of chewing and so tears off portions | It seeks to render itself dizzy | It is cleansing its prey | It is signalling to the other crocodiles that the prey is its own
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nearest living relatives of the crocodiles. | Birds | Snakes | Lizards | Frogs
